WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.498
Welcome to Grade 6 English, Unit 2: Sports and Health.

2
00:00:03.578 --> 00:00:11.316
Unit 2 pairs an information text on the science of sport
and health with the environmental story 'When the Leaves Stop

3
00:00:11.396 --> 00:00:11.978
Speaking',

4
00:00:12.058 --> 00:00:16.815
in which a young researcher named
Amal fights to protect a drying forest.

5
00:00:16.895 --> 00:00:24.368
Learners study twenty-eight precise words such as ecosystem,
transpiration, efficient, propaganda and restoration,

6
00:00:24.448 --> 00:00:29.271
and they master the First Conditional,
including 'unless' and modal verbs.

7
00:00:29.351 --> 00:00:36.691
They separate fact from opinion, then write a biography,
a sports commentary and persuasive pieces of their own.

8
00:00:36.771 --> 00:00:42.257
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:42.337 --> 00:00:50.255
By the end of this unit, you will be able to use and
understand 28 new vocabulary words linked to science,

10
00:00:50.335 --> 00:00:52.292
the environment and health.

11
00:00:52.372 --> 00:01:00.290
You will also identify and use the First Conditional
to talk about likely future events and their results.

12
00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:05.873
You will also use 'unless' and modal
verbs to shape conditional sentences.

13
00:01:05.953 --> 00:01:08.665
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:08.745 --> 00:01:15.584
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: geographic,
originally, imagination, agency, observation, deteriorate.

15
00:01:15.664 --> 00:01:20.416
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:20.496 --> 00:01:24.174
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:24.254 --> 00:01:29.904
The reading sequence includes Training Champions:
The Science Behind Sport and Reading Closely:

18
00:01:29.984 --> 00:01:32.015
Fact and Opinion in Health Writing.

19
00:01:32.095 --> 00:01:32.859
Read actively.

20
00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:37.021
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

21
00:01:37.101 --> 00:01:40.941
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

22
00:01:41.021 --> 00:01:44.934
The first language focus is What Is the First Conditional?

23
00:01:45.014 --> 00:01:51.955
The First Conditional describes real, likely situations
in the future and the result that will follow.

24
00:01:52.035 --> 00:01:56.912
We use it when there is a genuine
chance that the condition will happen.

25
00:01:56.992 --> 00:02:04.415
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

26
00:02:04.495 --> 00:02:08.920
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Sports Interview Role-Play.

27
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.232
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

28
00:02:13.312 --> 00:02:16.514
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

29
00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:19.924
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

30
00:02:20.004 --> 00:02:21.571
You are ready to begin.

31
00:02:21.651 --> 00:02:28.660
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

32
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.097
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

33
00:02:32.177 --> 00:02:40.045
Use AI English for hints and feedback any time, and bring
your questions to a live session too if you join one.
